.TH WDOCK 1
.SH NAME
wdock \- the desktop's dock in wsys
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wdock
[
.B -d
.I dock
] [
.B -f
.I font.ttf
] [
.B -s
.I size
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Wdock
runs in a window of
.IR wsys (4)
and makes it a bar along the bottom of the screen
(the
.B bar
message to
.BR /dev/wctl ,
as
.IR wbar (1)
along the top:
no border, above the other windows, the mouse but never the keyboard's focus;
a window maximized stays above it).
In its middle are starters, as many as fit: each a label and a command,
the lines of
.I dock
(default
.BR $home/lib/wdock/dock ,
else
.BR /lib/wdock/dock ):
a label, a tab, the command, perhaps a tab and a word;
lines starting with
.B #
are comments.
A starter's windows are those of
.B /dev/wsys
whose label starts with its word (without one, its label);
a dash below the label marks each of them (three at most),
the label is on the theme's selection colour when one of them is current,
paler when all of them are hidden.
.TP
Button 1
on a starter without windows runs its command with
.IR rc (1)
in
.IR wdock 's
name space (its
.B /dev
is its window's,
.IR window (1)
makes a new window);
on one with windows brings one up (current, on top, back when hidden);
on one whose window is current goes to the next of them,
or hides it when it is the only one.
.TP
Button 2
runs the command again: another one.
.PP
The colours are the theme's
.RB ( /dev/theme ,
.IR wsys (4)):
.B window
behind,
.B text
for the labels,
.B border
for a line along the top and the dashes,
.B select
for the current one.
The font is
.IR font.ttf ,
else the theme's
.BR uifont ,
else
.BR /lib/font/ttf/Go-Regular.ttf ,
.I size
pixels (15) at 96 dots per inch, at the window system's scale
.RI ( wsys (4)'s
.BR scale ,
its window's
.BR dpi )
as many times as big, as are the bar's sizes.
When
.I wsys
says the window changed (the screen's size, the theme),
it goes to the bottom of the screen again and reads the theme again.
It looks at the windows twice a second.
.PP
.I Riostart
does not start it;
a line of one's own
.B riostart
does, after
.IR wbar :
.IP
.EX
window -r 0 700 1024 768 wdock
.EE
.SH EXAMPLES
A dock of one's own: a terminal, acme (its windows' label
.BR acme ),
.IR stats (8):
.IP
.EX
term	window term
acme	window -r 40 40 1000 700 acme
stats	window -r 0 30 161 147 stats -lmisce
.EE
.SH FILES
.TF /lib/wdock/dock
.TP
.B /lib/wdock/dock
the starters by default
.SH SOURCE
.B /sys/src/cmd/wdock
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR wbar (1),
.IR wsys (4),
.IR winwatch (1),
.IR window (1)
.SH BUGS
Under
.IR rio (1),
which knows no bars, it is an ordinary window.
.br
A window is a starter's by its label only: one that changes its label
(a shell's
.IR term (1)
when a program sets the title) leaves it;
a window without a label (a shell of
.IR window (1))
is no starter's.
.br
.B WDOCKDEBUG=items
writes where its window and the starters are, with their windows' number and
state, to
.BI /tmp/wdock. pid .items
after each picture (for tests).
